(window.guideContent=window.guideContent||[]).push(["loops",{"l:loops/anatomy-of-the-agent-loop-in-code":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>An agent loop is a \u003ccode>for\u003c/code> loop around a model call. Each \u003cb>turn\u003c/b> sends the whole message list, gets back text and/or tool calls, runs the tools, appends the results and goes round again. It ends when the model replies without tool calls, or when your code stops it. Three details decide whether it is correct: the message list must stay well formed, every tool call needs exactly one result, and you must read the \u003cb>stop reason\u003c/b>.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>A complete loop\u003c/h4>\u003cp>This is a real, minimal implementation in the OpenAI-compatible Chat Completions shape, which most providers and local servers (Ollama, vLLM) accept:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>import json\nfrom openai import OpenAI\n\nclient = OpenAI()                    # or OpenAI(base_url=..., api_key=...) for another provider\nMODEL = \"&lt;model&gt;\"\n\ndef get_weather(city: str) -&gt; str:\n    return json.dumps({\"city\": city, \"temp_c\": 14, \"sky\": \"rain\"})   # stand-in for a real API\n\nTOOLS = [{\"type\": \"function\", \"function\": {\n    \"name\": \"get_weather\",\n    \"description\": \"Current weather for a city.\",\n    \"parameters\": {\"type\": \"object\",\n                   \"properties\": {\"city\": {\"type\": \"string\"}},\n                   \"required\": [\"city\"]}}}]\nREGISTRY = {\"get_weather\": get_weather}\n\ndef run_agent(task: str, max_steps: int = 10) -&gt; str:\n    messages = [{\"role\": \"system\", \"content\": \"Use tools when they help. Answer briefly.\"},\n                {\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": task}]\n    for _ in range(max_steps):\n        resp = client.chat.completions.create(model=MODEL, messages=messages, tools=TOOLS)\n        choice = resp.choices[0]\n        msg = choice.message\n        messages.append(msg.model_dump(exclude_none=True))   # keep the assistant turn as returned\n        if not msg.tool_calls:                               # no tool calls: the model is done\n            if choice.finish_reason == \"length\":\n                raise RuntimeError(\"answer cut off by max_tokens\")\n            return msg.content\n        for call in msg.tool_calls:                          # exactly one result per call id\n            fn = REGISTRY.get(call.function.name)\n            try:\n                args = json.loads(call.function.arguments or \"{}\")\n                result = fn(**args) if fn else f\"Error: no tool named {call.function.name}\"\n            except Exception as e:                           # errors become observations\n                result = f\"Error: {type(e).__name__}: {e}\"\n            messages.append({\"role\": \"tool\", \"tool_call_id\": call.id, \"content\": str(result)})\n    raise RuntimeError(f\"no final answer after {max_steps} steps\")\n\nprint(run_agent(\"Do I need an umbrella in Leeds today?\"))\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>What accumulates\u003c/h4>\u003cp>After one tool round the message list looks like this, and the whole list is sent on every turn:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">json\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>[\n  {\"role\": \"system\", \"content\": \"Use tools when they help. Answer briefly.\"},\n  {\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"Do I need an umbrella in Leeds today?\"},\n  {\"role\": \"assistant\", \"tool_calls\": [{\"id\": \"call_1\", \"type\": \"function\",\n    \"function\": {\"name\": \"get_weather\", \"arguments\": \"{\\\"city\\\": \\\"Leeds\\\"}\"}}]},\n  {\"role\": \"tool\", \"tool_call_id\": \"call_1\", \"content\": \"{\\\"city\\\": \\\"Leeds\\\", \\\"temp_c\\\": 14, \\\"sky\\\": \\\"rain\\\"}\"},\n  {\"role\": \"assistant\", \"content\": \"Yes: it is raining in Leeds, 14 degrees.\"}\n]\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The model is stateless: it only knows what is in this list. That is why input tokens grow every turn (see the Loop economics lesson) and why what you put into tool results matters so much.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Anthropic's Messages API has the same structure with different shapes: the assistant's content is a list of blocks including \u003ccode>tool_use\u003c/code> blocks, and results go back as \u003ccode>tool_result\u003c/code> blocks inside the next \u003ccode>user\u003c/code> message, matched by \u003ccode>tool_use_id\u003c/code>. If you use extended thinking with tools, the thinking blocks of the last assistant turn must be passed back unchanged.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Stop reasons\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Every response says why generation stopped. Read it.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Chat Completions \u003ccode>finish_reason\u003c/code>\u003c/th>\u003cth>Anthropic \u003ccode>stop_reason\u003c/code>\u003c/th>\u003cth>Meaning\u003c/th>\u003cth>What the loop should do\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>tool_calls\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>tool_use\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>The model wants tools run\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Run them, append results, continue\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>stop\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>end_turn\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>The model finished its turn\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Treat as a claim of completion\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>length\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>max_tokens\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Output hit the token limit\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Not an answer: raise the limit or fail; a cut-off tool call has broken JSON\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>content_filter\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>refusal\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Blocked or refused\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Surface it; do not retry blindly\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>none\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>pause_turn\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>A long server-side tool turn was paused\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Send the response back to let it continue\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>none\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>model_context_window_exceeded\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>The context window is full\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Compact or stop\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Two practical rules: decide \"done\" by the \u003cb>absence of tool calls\u003c/b>, because finish reasons are not reported consistently across every OpenAI-compatible server; and never treat a \u003ccode>length\u003c/code> stop as a finished answer.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Dispatch rules that keep the loop valid\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Append the assistant message first, exactly as returned\u003c/b>, including its tool calls; then one tool-result message per call id. A missing or extra result makes the next request fail with a 400 error.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Never let a tool raise into the loop.\u003c/b> Unknown tools, bad JSON and exceptions become short error strings the model can react to (the Errors lesson goes further).\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Several calls per turn are normal\u003c/b> (parallel tool calls). Read-only calls can run concurrently; calls that change state are safer run in order. The Claude Agent SDK does exactly this: read-only tools concurrently, edits and shell commands sequentially.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Some providers want \u003ccode>content\u003c/code> present\u003c/b> on assistant messages that only contain tool calls; if yours rejects the message, set it to an empty string.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Same loop inside the SDKs\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The \u003cb>OpenAI Agents SDK\u003c/b> runner calls the model; if the output is final (text of the expected type with no tool calls) it returns; if it is a handoff it switches agent; if it has tool calls it runs them and loops; past \u003ccode>max_turns\u003c/code> (default 10) it raises \u003ccode>MaxTurnsExceeded\u003c/code>. The \u003cb>Claude Agent SDK\u003c/b> runs Claude Code's loop and streams messages; the final \u003ccode>ResultMessage\u003c/code> has a \u003ccode>subtype\u003c/code> such as \u003ccode>success\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>error_max_turns\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>error_max_budget_usd\u003c/code>, plus cost and usage. A \"turn\" there is one model response plus its tool executions, so a task with three tool-using turns and a final answer takes four turns. Underneath, both are the 30 lines above.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why must the assistant message containing tool calls be appended before the tool results?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Tool results are only valid as answers to a specific assistant turn: each result's \u003ccode>tool_call_id\u003c/code> must match a call in the immediately preceding assistant message. Without that message the API rejects the request, and the model would not see what it had asked for.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">The model returns \u003ccode>finish_reason: \"length\"\u003c/code> with a half-written tool call. What should the loop do?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Not execute anything and not treat it as an answer. The arguments are truncated JSON. Retry the turn with a higher output limit (or ask for a smaller step), and if it keeps happening, fail with a clear error.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">In the Claude Agent SDK, how many turns does a task take if Claude makes tool calls in three responses and then answers, and why does that matter for \u003ccode>max_turns\u003c/code>?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Four turns: three with tool calls plus the final text-only response. With \u003ccode>max_turns\u003c/code> set to 3 the run would stop with \u003ccode>error_max_turns\u003c/code> before producing its answer.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","c:agent-harnesses-and-stopping-conditions":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>A \u003cb>harness\u003c/b> is everything around the model that turns it into a working agent: the loop and its stop rules, the tools, context management and compaction, state that survives sessions, permissions and sandboxing, hooks, verification and observability. The same model performs very differently in different harnesses, so \u003cb>harness engineering\u003c/b> (designing and tuning that scaffolding) is often a bigger lever than prompt wording.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>The parts of a harness\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Part\u003c/th>\u003cth>Job\u003c/th>\u003cth>In a coding agent\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Loop and stop rules\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Run turns; decide when to stop\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Turn and budget limits, done checks\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Tools (the agent-computer interface)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>What the agent can do\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Read, edit, shell, search, browser\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Context management\u003c/td>\u003ctd>What the model sees each turn\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Instruction files, compaction, clearing old tool output\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>State\u003c/td>\u003ctd>What survives turns and sessions\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Session log, plan and progress files, git history\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Permissions and sandbox\u003c/td>\u003ctd>What it may touch\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Permission modes, allowlists, containers\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Hooks\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Your code at lifecycle points\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Block \u003ccode>rm -rf\u003c/code> before it runs; run tests when the agent tries to stop\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Verification\u003c/td>\u003ctd>How work is checked\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Test runner, type checker, end-to-end browser tests\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Observability\u003c/td>\u003ctd>How people see what happened\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Traces, transcripts, cost per run\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Anthropic's evals guidance puts it plainly: when you evaluate \"an agent\", you evaluate the harness and the model together. That is why the same model can post very different SWE-bench scores in two reports.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Harness engineering beats prompt polishing\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Three pieces of evidence from Anthropic's published work:\u003c/p>\u003cul>\u003cli>Building their SWE-bench agent, they \u003cb>spent more time on tools than on the prompt\u003c/b>. The model kept making mistakes with relative file paths after changing directory; making the tool require absolute paths removed the problem.\u003c/li>\u003cli>In the long-running harness study, a frontier model in a loop with compaction still failed to build a production-quality web app from a one-line prompt. It tried to do everything at once, or declared victory early. The fix was harness structure (a feature list, one feature per session, a progress file, git commits, end-to-end tests), not a cleverer prompt.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Harnesses go stale.\u003c/b> They encode assumptions about what the model cannot do. One model wrapped up work prematurely as its context filled (\"context anxiety\"), so the harness added context resets; on a later model the behaviour was gone and the resets became dead weight. Re-test your harness when you change models.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Build, use an SDK, or use a managed harness\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Option\u003c/th>\u003cth>Examples (late 2026)\u003c/th>\u003cth>You control\u003c/th>\u003cth>You give up\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Your own loop\u003c/td>\u003ctd>30 to 300 lines on a model API\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Everything\u003c/td>\u003ctd>You build compaction, sandboxing, tracing yourself\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Agent SDK in your app\u003c/td>\u003ctd>OpenAI Agents SDK, Claude Agent SDK, Google ADK, Microsoft Agent Framework's Harness Agent, LangChain Deep Agents\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Tools, approvals, storage, deployment\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Some loop internals\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Managed harness\u003c/td>\u003ctd>OpenAI Agents API (a hosted Codex harness with compaction, multi-agent orchestration and sandboxes); Claude Managed Agents (a hosted harness with sessions and sandboxes)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Configuration and tools\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Runtime control, data location, portability\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Microsoft's Harness Agent description is a good summary of what \"batteries included\" means: planning and to-do tracking, context compaction, file access and memory, \"don't ask again\" tool approvals and observability. The Frameworks module compares the options in depth.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>A design lesson from managed harnesses\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Anthropic's Managed Agents separate three things: the \u003cb>session\u003c/b> (an append-only log of everything that happened), the \u003cb>harness\u003c/b> (the loop that calls the model and routes tool calls) and the \u003cb>sandbox\u003c/b> (where code runs). The harness calls the sandbox like any other tool, roughly \u003ccode>execute(name, input) -&gt; string\u003c/code>, so a dead container is just a tool error the model can react to. Because the log lives outside the harness, a crashed harness is replaced and resumes from the last event. Your own harness can copy this: write every event to a durable log as it happens, and make the loop restartable from that log.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Budgets every harness enforces\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Limit\u003c/th>\u003cth>Typical setting\u003c/th>\u003cth>How to check\u003c/th>\u003cth>When it is hit\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Steps (turns)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>10 to 30 interactive; 50 to 200 for long coding runs\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Count model calls\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Stop; return status \u003ccode>max_steps\u003c/code> with the partial result\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Tokens\u003c/td>\u003ctd>A per-run cap, such as 500,000 input tokens\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Sum \u003ccode>usage\u003c/code> from every response\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Compact the context, or stop with \u003ccode>max_tokens\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Time\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Seconds for chat, minutes for background jobs\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Wall clock before each turn; timeouts on every tool\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Cancel, save state, return \u003ccode>max_time\u003c/code> so it can resume\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Money\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Dollars per task\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Usage times price per token\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Stop with \u003ccode>max_usd\u003c/code>; alert if it happens often\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Each limit returns a \u003cb>distinct status\u003c/b>, never a fake answer, so the caller can decide: retry with a bigger budget, resume later, or hand to a human. Leave headroom: the Claude Agent SDK checks spend after each response arrives, so the response that crosses the cap still completes and is billed.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Set step, token and time budgets for an agent and say how you handle each limit.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>For an interactive support agent, for example: 20 steps, 300,000 total input tokens, 90 seconds wall clock, plus $0.30. Count model calls for steps, sum the usage field of every response for tokens, check elapsed time before each turn and put timeouts on every tool call. When the step limit is hit, stop and return \u003ccode>max_steps\u003c/code> with the work so far; on the token limit, compact older history once and stop if it is hit again; on the time limit, cancel running tools, save state and return \u003ccode>max_time\u003c/code> so the run can be resumed or escalated; on the dollar limit, stop with \u003ccode>max_usd\u003c/code> and alert. Never return a made-up final answer when a limit is hit.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why can the same model score very differently on an agent benchmark in two reports?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Agent benchmarks measure the model and its harness together. Different tools, prompts, context management, retries and verification steps change the score even when the model is identical.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">What is the benefit of keeping the session log outside the harness process?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The harness becomes disposable: if it crashes, a new one reads the log and resumes from the last event, and the log doubles as a complete trace for debugging and evals.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","l:loops/stopping-conditions-and-budgets":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>An agent should stop for one of four reasons, and your harness must tell them apart: \u003cb>done\u003c/b> (a checkable success criterion is met), \u003cb>blocked\u003c/b> (it needs a person or a missing resource), \u003cb>out of budget\u003c/b> (steps, tokens, time or money) or \u003cb>stuck\u003c/b> (no progress over several turns). \"The model stopped calling tools\" is only a \u003ci>claim\u003c/i> of done. Verify it.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Four reasons to stop\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Reason\u003c/th>\u003cth>Detected by\u003c/th>\u003cth>Return to the caller\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Done\u003c/td>\u003ctd>An external check passes: tests, validator, database state\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>done\u003c/code> plus the result and evidence\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Blocked\u003c/td>\u003ctd>The agent calls \u003ccode>ask_human\u003c/code>, or a required permission or resource is missing\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>blocked\u003c/code> plus the question\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Out of budget\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Your counters for steps, tokens, time or money\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>max_steps\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>max_usd\u003c/code> and so on, plus partial work\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Stuck\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Repeated identical calls, no change in the environment, the same failure again and again\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>stuck\u003c/code> plus the evidence\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Make \"done\" machine-checkable\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Good done criteria are things code can test: \u003ccode>pytest\u003c/code> exits with 0; a refund row with the right amount exists; the output validates against a schema; every item in a feature list has \u003ccode>\"passes\": true\u003c/code>. Claude Code's guidance is blunt about why: the agent stops when the work \u003ci>looks\u003c/i> done, and if it has nothing that comes back pass or fail, \"looks done\" is all it has.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The pattern is a \u003cb>done check\u003c/b> that runs when the model stops calling tools. If the check fails, the harness sends the evidence back and lets it continue, a bounded number of times:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def run_with_done_check(messages: list, check, max_rejections: int = 3) -&gt; dict:\n    for _ in range(max_rejections + 1):\n        answer = agent_loop(messages)       # runs until the model replies with no tool calls\n        ok, report = check()                # e.g. run pytest, query the database\n        if ok:\n            return {\"status\": \"done\", \"answer\": answer, \"evidence\": report[-500:]}\n        messages.append({\"role\": \"user\", \"content\":\n                         f\"Not done yet. The check failed:\\n{report[-2000:]}\\nKeep working.\"})\n    return {\"status\": \"stuck\", \"answer\": answer, \"evidence\": report[-2000:]}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Claude Code builds this into the harness as a \u003cb>Stop hook\u003c/b>: a script runs whenever the agent tries to end its turn, and if it exits with code 2, its stderr goes back to the agent as the reason it cannot stop yet:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">json\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>{\n  \"hooks\": {\n    \"Stop\": [\n      {\"hooks\": [{\"type\": \"command\", \"command\": \"pnpm tsc --noEmit 1&gt;&amp;2 || exit 2\"}]}\n    ]\n  }\n}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The hook has its own cap (by default it gives up after blocking eight stops in a row with no tool call in between), which is the same \"bounded rejections\" idea.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Budget accounting\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>import time\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass, field\n\n@dataclass\nclass Budget:\n    max_steps: int = 30\n    max_tokens: int = 400_000\n    max_seconds: float = 300\n    max_usd: float = 0.50\n    steps: int = 0\n    tokens: int = 0\n    usd: float = 0.0\n    started: float = field(default_factory=time.monotonic)\n\n    def charge(self, usage, in_price: float, out_price: float) -&gt; None:\n        \"\"\"Prices are per million tokens; ignoring cache discounts over-estimates, which is safe.\"\"\"\n        self.steps += 1\n        self.tokens += usage.prompt_tokens + usage.completion_tokens\n        self.usd += (usage.prompt_tokens * in_price + usage.completion_tokens * out_price) / 1e6\n\n    def exceeded(self) -&gt; str | None:\n        if self.steps &gt;= self.max_steps: return \"max_steps\"\n        if self.tokens &gt;= self.max_tokens: return \"max_tokens\"\n        if time.monotonic() - self.started &gt;= self.max_seconds: return \"max_time\"\n        if self.usd &gt;= self.max_usd: return \"max_usd\"\n        return None\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Call \u003ccode>charge\u003c/code> after every model response and \u003ccode>exceeded\u003c/code> before the next one. To avoid overshooting, also stop when the remaining money is less than the last turn's cost (the next turn will cost at least as much, because the context only grows).\u003c/p>\u003ch4>No-progress detection\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Budgets catch runaway loops eventually; progress checks catch them early. Useful signals:\u003c/p>\u003cul>\u003cli>The \u003cb>same tool with the same arguments\u003c/b> called three or more times.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>No change in the environment\u003c/b> for several turns: \u003ccode>git diff\u003c/code> unchanged, no new rows, no new files.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>The failure count is not falling\u003c/b>: the same tests fail across three verify cycles.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Oscillation\u003c/b>: edit A, then undo A, then A again.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp>Respond in two stages: first a nudge appended to the tool result (\"You have run this exact search three times with the same result; try a different approach or call ask_human\"), then, if it continues, stop with \u003ccode>stuck\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Choosing the numbers\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Set budgets from data, not instinct. Run your eval set and look at successful runs. Say they need a median of 8 steps and 14 at the 95th percentile; a cap of 20 loses almost no successes, because runs that go far beyond the successful range are nearly always failing. Do the same for cost: a cap of two to three times the median successful cost is a common starting point. Revisit the numbers when you change model or tools.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">The model replies with no tool calls, saying \"All fixed!\", but the tests still fail. What should the harness do?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Treat the reply as an unverified claim: run the done check, send the failing output back as a new message telling it to continue, and allow this only a few times before stopping with a \u003ccode>stuck\u003c/code> status and the evidence. It must not report success.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">How do you choose a value for \u003ccode>max_steps\u003c/code>?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Run the agent on an eval set, look at the distribution of steps in successful runs, and set the cap a little above the high end (for example 20 when the 95th percentile is 14), since runs far beyond that are almost always failing. Re-measure when the model or tools change.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Name two signals that an agent is stuck rather than slow.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Any two of: repeated identical tool calls with identical results; no change to the environment (files, git diff, database) over several turns; the same tests failing across several verify cycles; edits that undo and redo each other.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","l:loops/errors-inside-the-loop":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>Inside an agent loop, most errors should become \u003cb>observations\u003c/b>: the tool returns a short, specific message and the model tries something else. The harness handles the rest: retries for transient failures, limits on repeated failures, and detection of \u003cb>doom loops\u003c/b>, where the agent keeps repeating an action that fails. Crashing on the first error and retrying forever are both wrong.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Three classes of error\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Class\u003c/th>\u003cth>Examples\u003c/th>\u003cth>Who handles it\u003c/th>\u003cth>How\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Transient infrastructure\u003c/td>\u003ctd>429 rate limit, 5xx, network timeout on the model API or an upstream service\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Harness\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Retry with exponential backoff and jitter; the model never sees it\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Fixable by the model\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Bad arguments, file not found, SQL syntax error, failing test\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Model\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Return a clear error as the tool result\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Fatal or policy\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Invalid credentials, budget exceeded, sandbox gone, repeated permission denials\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Harness\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Stop with a status; escalate\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Errors as observations: write them for the model\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Compare two results for the same mistake:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>Unhelpful:  Traceback (most recent call last): ... KeyError: 'cust_id'\nHelpful:    Error: unknown field 'cust_id'. Valid fields: customer_id, email, created_at.\n            Example: search_customers(field=\"customer_id\", value=\"C-1042\")\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The second tells the model what went wrong and exactly how to fix it, in a few tokens. Anthropic's tool-writing guidance says the same: prompt-engineer error responses to communicate specific, actionable improvements rather than opaque error codes or tracebacks. Error messages are prompts.\u003c/p>\u003cp>A wrapper that guarantees no tool can crash the loop:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>import logging\nfrom concurrent.futures import ThreadPoolExecutor\n\nlog = logging.getLogger(\"agent\")\nPOOL = ThreadPoolExecutor(max_workers=4)\n\ndef truncate(text: str, limit: int = 4000) -&gt; str:\n    if len(text) &lt;= limit:\n        return text\n    half = limit // 2\n    return text[:half] + f\"\\n... [{len(text) - limit} characters cut] ...\\n\" + text[-half:]\n\ndef safe_call(fn, args: dict, timeout_s: float = 30) -&gt; str:\n    try:\n        return truncate(str(POOL.submit(fn, **args).result(timeout=timeout_s)))\n    except TimeoutError:\n        return f\"Error: {fn.__name__} timed out after {timeout_s}s. Try a narrower request.\"\n    except (ValueError, KeyError, FileNotFoundError, TypeError) as e:\n        return f\"Error: {e}\"          # tool authors raise messages written for the model\n    except Exception as e:\n        log.exception(\"tool %s crashed\", fn.__name__)   # full traceback for you, not the model\n        return f\"Error: {fn.__name__} failed unexpectedly ({type(e).__name__}). Try another approach.\"\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>A thread that times out keeps running in the background, so tools that start processes should also pass their own timeout (for example \u003ccode>subprocess.run(..., timeout=60)\u003c/code>).\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Retries belong in the harness\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>import random, time\nfrom openai import APIConnectionError, APITimeoutError, InternalServerError, RateLimitError\n\ndef create_with_retry(client, max_attempts: int = 5, **kw):\n    for attempt in range(max_attempts):\n        try:\n            return client.chat.completions.create(**kw)\n        except (RateLimitError, APITimeoutError, APIConnectionError, InternalServerError):\n            if attempt == max_attempts - 1:\n                raise\n            time.sleep(min(30, 2 ** attempt) + random.random())   # 1, 2, 4, 8 s plus jitter\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The OpenAI Python SDK already retries some failures (twice by default), so tune \u003ccode>max_retries\u003c/code> rather than stacking two retry layers blindly. Only retry tools that \u003cb>change state\u003c/b> when they are idempotent: a payment call retried after a timeout must carry the same idempotency key, or you may pay twice.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Doom loops\u003c/h4>\u003cp>A \u003cb>doom loop\u003c/b> is an agent repeating the same failing action, or oscillating between two, while the context fills with near-identical errors and the bill grows. Typical causes: a misleading error message, a missing capability (the tool it needs does not exist), a wrong belief it never re-checks, or a test it cannot pass.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>import hashlib, json\nfrom collections import Counter\n\nclass LoopGuard:\n    def __init__(self, max_repeats: int = 3, max_errors_in_a_row: int = 5):\n        self.seen = Counter()\n        self.errors_in_a_row = 0\n        self.max_repeats, self.max_errors = max_repeats, max_errors_in_a_row\n\n    def check(self, name: str, args: dict, result: str) -&gt; str | None:\n        key = hashlib.sha256(f\"{name}:{json.dumps(args, sort_keys=True)}\".encode()).hexdigest()\n        self.seen[key] += 1\n        self.errors_in_a_row = self.errors_in_a_row + 1 if result.startswith(\"Error\") else 0\n        if self.seen[key] &gt; self.max_repeats:\n            return f\"{name} called {self.seen[key]} times with identical arguments\"\n        if self.errors_in_a_row &gt;= self.max_errors:\n            return f\"{self.errors_in_a_row} tool errors in a row\"\n        return None\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Call \u003ccode>check\u003c/code> after every tool call. Respond with a ladder: the \u003cb>first\u003c/b> time it fires, append a note to the tool result (\"You have made this exact call several times with the same outcome. Change approach or call ask_human.\"); the \u003cb>second\u003c/b> time, stop the run with status \u003ccode>stuck\u003c/code>; and \u003cb>always\u003c/b> record the event, because recurring doom loops are a harness bug to fix (Outer loops lesson).\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Keep errors from flooding the context\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Twenty stack traces in the history push out useful context and anchor the model on old failures. Keep the latest failure in full and shrink older ones to one line (\"attempt 3: same AssertionError in test_slugify\"), truncate long outputs from the middle so the start and end survive, and never echo secrets from environment variables or configuration into error text.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">An upstream search API sometimes returns HTTP 503. Should the model see this error?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Not at first. Retry inside the tool or harness with backoff two or three times, because the model cannot fix a transient outage. If it still fails, return a short observation such as \"Error: search is temporarily unavailable; try again later or answer from the documents already retrieved\", so the model can choose another route.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Rewrite this tool result to be useful to the model: \u003ccode>psycopg.errors.UndefinedColumn: column \"cust_id\" does not exist\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Something like: \"Error: column 'cust_id' does not exist in table orders. Available columns: order_id, customer_id, status, total, created_at. Did you mean customer_id?\" It names the problem, lists valid options and suggests the fix in a few tokens.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">What is a doom loop, and how can a harness detect one?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>An agent repeating the same failing action (or oscillating) without progress while cost grows. Detect it by hashing tool name and arguments to count identical calls, counting consecutive errors, and checking whether the environment (diff, test failures) has changed over several turns; nudge once, then stop with \u003ccode>stuck\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","l:loops/verification-loops":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>A \u003cb>verification loop\u003c/b> generates something, checks it with an independent signal, and feeds failures back until the check passes or a limit is reached. The check sets the ceiling: with fast, trustworthy checks such as tests and type checkers, even a weak first attempt converges; without them the agent can only guess. That is why verification is the biggest single quality lever in agent design, and why coding is where agents work best.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>The loop\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"flow\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Diagram\">\u003cdiv class=\"fr\">\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Generate\u003c/b>\u003csmall>code, query, answer\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Check\u003c/b>\u003csmall>tests, types, validator, judge\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Pass\u003c/b>\u003csmall>done, with evidence\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"fr\">\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Check\u003c/b>\u003csmall>tests, types, validator, judge\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Fail\u003c/b>\u003csmall>precise errors fed back\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Generate again\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Anthropic's appendix on coding agents names the reason they work so well: code solutions are verifiable through automated tests, and agents can iterate using test results as feedback. Geoffrey Huntley calls these checks \u003cb>backpressure\u003c/b>: anything that pushes back on bad output before it is accepted.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>A ladder of checks\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Check\u003c/th>\u003cth>Speed\u003c/th>\u003cth>Trust\u003c/th>\u003cth>Catches\u003c/th>\u003cth>Example\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Schema validation\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Milliseconds\u003c/td>\u003ctd>High\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Wrong shape or types\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Pydantic, JSON Schema\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Linter and formatter\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Milliseconds to seconds\u003c/td>\u003ctd>High\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Style, many simple bugs\u003c/td>\u003ctd>ruff, eslint\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Type checker\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Seconds\u003c/td>\u003ctd>High\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Interface mistakes\u003c/td>\u003ctd>mypy, pyright, pyrefly, tsc\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Unit tests\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Seconds\u003c/td>\u003ctd>As good as the tests\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Logic errors\u003c/td>\u003ctd>pytest\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Execution\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Seconds\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Medium\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Runtime errors, empty results\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Run the script; run the SQL and look at rows\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>End-to-end tests\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Seconds to minutes\u003c/td>\u003ctd>High\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Integration and UI bugs\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Playwright or Puppeteer driving the real app\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Reference checks\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Milliseconds\u003c/td>\u003ctd>High\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Invented citations or quotes\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Quote must appear verbatim in the source\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Model judge with a rubric\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Seconds\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Medium\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Subjective quality\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Separate, sceptical grader\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Human review\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Hours\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Highest\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Wrong intent\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Pull request review\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Two rules: use the \u003cb>deterministic checks first\u003c/b> and a judge only for what is left; and run \u003cb>fast checks inside the loop\u003c/b>, slow ones at milestones. Huntley stresses that the wheel must turn fast: after each change, run the tests for the unit that changed, and the full suite before a commit. For dynamically typed languages he insists on wiring in a type checker, because otherwise there is too little backpressure.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Feed failures back well\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The model can only fix what the check output shows. Make it \u003cb>specific\u003c/b> (test name, expected versus actual, file and line), \u003cb>short\u003c/b> (first failures and the tail of the log, not 5,000 lines) and \u003cb>current\u003c/b> (only the latest run in full):\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>import subprocess\n\ndef run_tests(path: str = \"tests\") -&gt; tuple[bool, str]:\n    p = subprocess.run([\"python\", \"-m\", \"pytest\", \"-q\", \"--tb=short\", path],\n                       capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=300)\n    return p.returncode == 0, (p.stdout + p.stderr)[-3000:]\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Anthropic's long-running harness found unit tests and \u003ccode>curl\u003c/code> calls were not enough: the agent kept marking features complete that did not work end to end. Giving it browser automation and telling it to test as a human user would \"dramatically improved performance\", because it could see bugs not obvious from the code.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Guard the check against gaming\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Models are trained to chase rewards, and passing tests looks like a reward. Left unguarded, agents sometimes delete or skip failing tests, special-case the test inputs, write placeholder implementations or edit the success criteria. Huntley's build prompt carries the sign \"DO NOT IMPLEMENT PLACEHOLDER OR SIMPLE IMPLEMENTATIONS\"; Anthropic's harness told agents that removing or editing tests was unacceptable, and kept the feature list in JSON because models were less likely to rewrite JSON than Markdown. Defences in the harness, not just the prompt:\u003c/p>\u003cul>\u003cli>Keep \u003cb>acceptance tests the agent cannot write to\u003c/b> (outside its writable directory, or checked by a hash after each iteration).\u003c/li>\u003cli>Run \u003cb>hidden tests\u003c/b> the agent never sees, as a final check.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Scan diffs for \u003ccode>skip\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>xfail\u003c/code>, \u003ccode># type: ignore\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>assert True\u003c/code> and deleted test files.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Use a \u003cb>separate reviewer\u003c/b> (a fresh subagent or a different model) that never saw the reasoning behind the change.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Self-evaluation is not verification\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Asking the generating agent \"is this good?\" is weak. Anthropic's harness-design work found agents grading their own output tend to praise it confidently, even when it is mediocre, and that a separate evaluator tuned to be sceptical is far more tractable. When quality is subjective (design, writing), turn taste into numbered criteria the evaluator can grade, as the Agents module's evaluator-optimiser lesson shows.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why is a fast, reliable test suite worth more to a coding agent than a better prompt?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The tests give the agent ground truth after every change, so it can find and fix its own mistakes and you can trust its \"done\". A better prompt makes the first attempt somewhat better, but without a check the agent cannot tell a wrong attempt from a right one, and errors survive until a human finds them.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">In what order would you put checks for an agent that writes SQL for analysts?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>First parse or explain the query (syntax and referenced columns exist), then run it with a row limit and timeout on a read replica (runtime errors, empty or huge results), then compare against known answers for test questions, and only then a model judge or analyst review on whether the result answers the question.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">The agent \"fixes\" a failing test by adding \u003ccode>@pytest.mark.skip\u003c/code>. How do you prevent this?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Make the acceptance tests read-only to the agent (or hash them and fail the iteration if they change), scan diffs for skip and xfail markers, run hidden tests as a final check, and add an explicit rule in the prompt that tests may never be removed or weakened.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","l:loops/the-ralph-loop":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>The \u003cb>Ralph loop\u003c/b>, named by Geoffrey Huntley after the Simpsons character Ralph Wiggum, is the simplest long-running harness: a shell loop that starts a fresh coding-agent session with the same prompt file, over and over: \u003ccode>while :; do cat PROMPT.md | claude-code ; done\u003c/code>. Each iteration reads the specs and a plan file, does \u003cb>one\u003c/b> item, runs the tests, updates the plan and commits. The skill is not the loop. It is \u003cb>tuning Ralph\u003c/b>: watching it fail and adding \"signs\" (instructions, tests, checks) until it fails less.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Why a dumb loop works\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>A fresh context every iteration.\u003c/b> There is no long conversation to rot or compact. Huntley's phrase is to \"deterministically allocate the stack the same way every loop\": the same prompt, specs and plan are loaded each time.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>State lives on disk, not in the chat.\u003c/b> The plan file, specs, instruction file and git history carry everything forward.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>One item per loop.\u003c/b> Each session stays within the part of the context window where the model works well. Huntley observed that the more of the window you use, the worse the results, and repeats \"one item per loop\" for emphasis.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Backpressure.\u003c/b> Builds, type checks and tests reject bad work every iteration.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Predictable failures.\u003c/b> In his words, the technique is \"deterministically bad in an undeterministic world\": its failure modes repeat, so you can fix them with signs.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>The files\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>project/\n  PROMPT.md       identical instructions for every iteration\n  fix_plan.md     prioritised to-do list that the agent maintains\n  AGENTS.md       how to build, run and test; short learnings (Huntley calls it AGENT.md)\n  specs/          one Markdown spec per feature, written with the agent before looping\n  acceptance/     human-written acceptance tests the agent may not edit\n  src/  tests/    code and the agent's own unit tests\n  ralph.sh        the loop with guardrails\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Specs come first. Huntley's approach is to have a long conversation with the agent about requirements, then ask it to write one spec file per topic. A wrong spec is expensive: a month into his project he noticed that his lexer spec defined one keyword twice for two opposing cases, and a lot of loop time had been wasted on it.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>PROMPT.md\u003c/h4>\u003cp>A minimal build prompt in the structure Huntley uses (study the inputs, do one thing, verify, record, commit), adapted for a Python project:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>0a. Study specs/* to learn the requirements.\n0b. Study fix_plan.md. It may be out of date.\n0c. Source code is in src/, unit tests in tests/, acceptance tests in acceptance/.\n    Build and test commands are in AGENTS.md.\n\n1. Choose the single most important unfinished item in fix_plan.md and implement it fully.\n   Before writing code, search the codebase: do not assume something is not implemented.\n2. Write unit tests for it. Run the tests for the code you changed, then the whole suite:\n   `pytest -q`. If tests unrelated to your change fail, fixing them is part of this task.\n3. When all tests pass: update fix_plan.md (mark the item done, add any bugs or gaps you found),\n   then run `git add -A` and `git commit -m \"&lt;what you did and why&gt;\"`.\n4. If you learn a better way to build or test, add one short line to AGENTS.md.\n\nRules:\n- One item per run. Then stop.\n- No placeholder or stub implementations. Implement fully, or leave the item open with a note.\n- Never edit acceptance/ and never delete, skip or weaken a test to make it pass.\n- In docstrings for tests, say why the test exists; future runs will not have your reasoning.\n- If every item in fix_plan.md is done and all tests pass, create an empty file named DONE.\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The last-but-one rule is one of Huntley's: each iteration starts with no memory, so notes explaining \u003ci>why\u003c/i> a test matters help later iterations decide whether to fix, change or keep it.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>fix_plan.md\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode># Fix plan (highest priority first)\n- [ ] `stats` command: totals per author (specs/stats.md)\n- [ ] Parse `--since` in ISO and relative forms such as \"3 days\" (specs/cli.md#since)\n- [ ] Bug: empty repository crashes `log` (found in iteration 7)\n- [x] Project skeleton with pytest and ruff configured\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Huntley runs a separate \u003cb>planning prompt\u003c/b> to create or rebuild this list: study the specs and the code, search for TODOs, placeholders and minimal implementations, and write a prioritised list of what is missing. He deletes and regenerates the list when it drifts, then switches back to the build prompt.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>ralph.sh with guardrails\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Huntley's one-liner is the essence; this version adds the limits an unattended run needs. It assumes the Claude Code CLI in print mode (\u003ccode>claude -p\u003c/code>); any agent CLI with a non-interactive mode works.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">bash\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# Run inside a disposable container or VM with only the project mounted. Never on your laptop's home directory.\nset -uo pipefail\nMAX_ITERS=${MAX_ITERS:-30}            # hard cap on iterations\nITER_TIMEOUT=${ITER_TIMEOUT:-20m}     # wall-clock cap per iteration\nITER_BUDGET=${ITER_BUDGET:-2}         # dollars per iteration\nmkdir -p logs\nacceptance_hash() { find acceptance -type f -print0 | sort -z | xargs -0 sha256sum | sha256sum; }\nbaseline=$(acceptance_hash)\nidle=0\n\nfor i in $(seq 1 \"$MAX_ITERS\"); do\n  [ -f STOP ] &amp;&amp; { echo \"STOP file found\" | tee -a logs/ralph.log; break; }     # kill switch\n  [ -f DONE ] &amp;&amp; { echo \"agent reports DONE\" | tee -a logs/ralph.log; break; }\n  before=$(git rev-parse HEAD)\n  echo \"=== iteration $i $(date -Is)\" | tee -a logs/ralph.log\n\n  timeout \"$ITER_TIMEOUT\" claude -p \"$(cat PROMPT.md)\" \\\n    --permission-mode acceptEdits \\\n    --allowedTools \"Bash(pytest *)\" \"Bash(ruff *)\" \"Bash(git add *)\" \"Bash(git commit *)\" \\\n    --max-turns 60 --max-budget-usd \"$ITER_BUDGET\" \\\n    --output-format stream-json --verbose &gt; \"logs/iter-$i.jsonl\" 2&gt;&amp;1\n  echo \"agent exit code $?\" | tee -a logs/ralph.log\n\n  if [ \"$(acceptance_hash)\" != \"$baseline\" ]; then\n    echo \"acceptance/ was modified: stopping for review\" | tee -a logs/ralph.log; break\n  fi\n  if pytest -q acceptance tests &gt; \"logs/tests-$i.txt\" 2&gt;&amp;1; then\n    echo \"tests pass\" | tee -a logs/ralph.log\n  else\n    echo \"tests FAIL (see logs/tests-$i.txt)\" | tee -a logs/ralph.log\n  fi\n  if [ \"$(git rev-parse HEAD)\" = \"$before\" ]; then idle=$((idle + 1)); else idle=0; fi\n  [ \"$idle\" -ge 3 ] &amp;&amp; { echo \"no commit in 3 iterations: stuck\" | tee -a logs/ralph.log; break; }\ndone\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>What each guardrail does: \u003ccode>MAX_ITERS\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>timeout\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>--max-budget-usd\u003c/code> bound total cost and time; \u003ccode>--max-turns\u003c/code> bounds one session; \u003ccode>acceptEdits\u003c/code> plus a short \u003ccode>--allowedTools\u003c/code> list means file edits are allowed but the only shell commands that run are tests, the linter and git add and commit (others are denied, because nobody is there to approve them); the \u003ccode>STOP\u003c/code> file is a kill switch you can create from outside; the acceptance-test hash stops the run if the agent edits the tests that judge it; the idle counter stops a loop that has stopped making commits; and the per-iteration JSON logs are your traces. With OpenAI's Codex CLI the equivalent call is \u003ccode>codex exec --sandbox workspace-write \"$(cat PROMPT.md)\"\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Tuning Ralph\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Watch the logs, look for patterns of bad behaviour, and add a sign for each. Common ones from Huntley's post:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Failure you see\u003c/th>\u003cth>Sign to add\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Re-implements something that exists\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\"Before making changes, search the codebase; do not assume it is not implemented.\"\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Placeholder or minimal implementations\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\"No placeholder implementations\"; plus a planning run that hunts for them\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Keeps rediscovering how to build\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\"When you learn how to run something, add it briefly to AGENTS.md\"\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Bugs found but forgotten\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\"Document every bug you notice in fix_plan.md, even if unrelated\"\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Plan file bloats\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\"When fix_plan.md gets long, remove completed items\"\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Wrong patterns or libraries\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Update specs or a standards document the prompt points to\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>He numbers later signs with ever larger runs of 9s (\"999.\", \"9999.\") to mark them as increasingly important. Expect to wake up to a broken build sometimes; the judgement call is whether to \u003ccode>git reset --hard\u003c/code> and restart or to write prompts that rescue it.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Where it works and where it fails\u003c/h4>\u003cp>It works best for \u003cb>greenfield projects\u003c/b> with clear specs and strong backpressure: Huntley used it to build a new programming language and expected to get about 90% of the way, with senior engineers steering. He also said he would not use it on an existing codebase. It struggles with vague requirements, weak tests (it will happily build the wrong thing quickly), subjective quality, and anything needing judgement about a large existing system. And it burns tokens by design: every iteration re-reads the specs and plan.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why does Ralph start a fresh session each iteration instead of one long conversation?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>A fresh context avoids context rot and lossy compaction: every iteration loads the same specs and plan in the same way and does one item within the part of the window where the model works best. Continuity comes from files on disk (plan, specs, AGENTS.md, git history), which are explicit, inspectable and editable by you.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">What does \"tuning Ralph with signs\" mean? Give an example.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Observing a recurring failure in the loop's output and adding an instruction, test or check that prevents it. For example, if Ralph re-implements functions that already exist because a search missed them, add \"Before making changes, search the codebase; do not assume an item is not implemented.\"\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Name four guardrails you would add before leaving a Ralph loop running overnight.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Any four of: a sandbox or container with only the project mounted; a maximum number of iterations and a per-iteration timeout; a dollar budget per iteration and overall; a short allowlist of shell commands; a STOP file kill switch; acceptance tests the agent cannot modify, verified by hash; a stuck detector such as no commit in three iterations; per-iteration logs.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","l:loops/long-running-harnesses":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>A long-running agent works in \u003cb>sessions\u003c/b>, and each new session starts with no memory, like an engineer arriving for a shift with no handover. A good harness makes the handover explicit: a first \u003cb>initializer\u003c/b> session sets up the environment, a \u003cb>feature list\u003c/b> and a \u003cb>progress file\u003c/b>; every later session gets its bearings from those files and git, does \u003cb>one feature\u003c/b>, tests it end to end, commits and leaves the code in a clean state.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Why \"a strong model plus compaction\" is not enough\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Anthropic ran a frontier model in a loop on the Claude Agent SDK, which compacts context automatically, and asked it to build a clone of a chat web app from a one-line prompt. It failed in four recurring ways:\u003c/p>\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cb>Trying to one-shot the app.\u003c/b> It ran out of context mid-feature, leaving the next session a half-built, undocumented mess to reverse-engineer.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Declaring victory early.\u003c/b> A later session saw that some features existed and decided the job was done.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Marking features done without real testing.\u003c/b> Unit tests and \u003ccode>curl\u003c/code> calls passed while the feature did not work for a user.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Wasting each session rediscovering\u003c/b> how to start the app and whether it currently worked.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cp>Compaction did not fix these, because a summary does not always pass perfectly clear instructions to the next session.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The two-part harness\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"flow\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Diagram\">\u003cdiv class=\"fr\">\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Spec\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Initializer session\u003c/b>\u003csmall>feature list, init.sh, progress file, first commit\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"fr\">\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Coding session\u003c/b>\u003csmall>get bearings\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Smoke test\u003c/b>\u003csmall>fix if broken\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>One feature\u003c/b>\u003csmall>implement, test end to end\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Commit and log progress\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Next session\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\u003cp>\u003cb>The initializer\u003c/b> (first session, its own prompt) expands the spec into a comprehensive feature list (over 200 entries for the chat-app clone), all marked failing; writes \u003ccode>init.sh\u003c/code> to start the development server; creates \u003ccode>claude-progress.txt\u003c/code>; and makes an initial git commit.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>The coding agent\u003c/b> (every later session, a different prompt) follows a fixed routine:\u003c/p>\u003col>\u003cli>Run \u003ccode>pwd\u003c/code>; read the git log and the progress file.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Read the feature list and pick the highest-priority feature that does not pass.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Run \u003ccode>init.sh\u003c/code> and a basic end-to-end test. If the app is broken, fix that first.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Implement \u003cb>one\u003c/b> feature and test it the way a user would (Anthropic gave it browser automation through a Puppeteer MCP server).\u003c/li>\u003cli>Change only that feature's \u003ccode>passes\u003c/code> field, commit with a descriptive message and append a summary to the progress file.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003ch4>The artefacts\u003c/h4>\u003cp>A feature list entry, in the style the article describes:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">json\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>{\n  \"category\": \"functional\",\n  \"description\": \"New chat button creates a fresh, empty conversation\",\n  \"steps\": [\"Open the app\", \"Click 'New chat'\", \"Check the message list is empty\",\n            \"Send a message and check a reply appears\"],\n  \"passes\": false\n}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The agent may only flip \u003ccode>passes\u003c/code>, under strongly worded instructions such as \"It is unacceptable to remove or edit tests because this could lead to missing or buggy functionality\". The team chose \u003cb>JSON over Markdown\u003c/b> because the model was less likely to rewrite JSON inappropriately. \u003cb>Git is the checkpoint system\u003c/b>: descriptive commits let a later session understand what changed and \u003ccode>git revert\u003c/code> a bad change. The \u003cb>progress file\u003c/b> is the human-readable handover note.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>A driver for the sessions\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The outer driver is plain code. This sketch runs each session as a Claude Code print-mode call; the guard refuses any change to the feature list other than \u003ccode>passes\u003c/code> flips:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>import json, subprocess\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\nFEATURES = Path(\"feature_list.json\")\n\ndef run_session(prompt_file: str) -&gt; int:\n    prompt = Path(prompt_file).read_text()\n    return subprocess.run([\"claude\", \"-p\", prompt, \"--permission-mode\", \"acceptEdits\",\n                           \"--max-turns\", \"120\", \"--max-budget-usd\", \"5\"], timeout=3600).returncode\n\ndef snapshot() -&gt; list[dict]:\n    return json.loads(FEATURES.read_text())\n\nif not FEATURES.exists():\n    run_session(\"prompts/initializer.md\")\n\nfor session in range(1, 101):\n    before = snapshot()\n    if all(f[\"passes\"] for f in before):\n        print(\"all features pass\"); break\n    run_session(\"prompts/coding.md\")\n    after = snapshot()\n    strip = lambda fs: [{k: v for k, v in f.items() if k != \"passes\"} for f in fs]\n    if strip(after) != strip(before):                     # the agent edited the requirements\n        subprocess.run([\"git\", \"checkout\", \"HEAD~1\", \"--\", str(FEATURES)])\n        print(f\"session {session}: feature list tampered with; restored and stopping\"); break\n    gained = sum(f[\"passes\"] for f in after) - sum(f[\"passes\"] for f in before)\n    print(f\"session {session}: {gained} feature(s) newly passing\")\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>(Restoring from \u003ccode>HEAD~1\u003c/code> assumes the session made one commit; a real driver would record the commit hash before each session.)\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Resets versus compaction\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Anthropic's later harness-design work distinguishes two ways to continue past a full context. \u003cb>Compaction\u003c/b> summarises earlier history in place so the same session continues; it keeps continuity but not a clean slate. A \u003cb>context reset\u003c/b> starts a fresh agent with a structured handoff file; it gives a clean slate but the handoff must carry enough state. Resets suited a model that showed \"context anxiety\" (wrapping up early as its context filled); with a later model the behaviour disappeared and resets became unnecessary overhead. Ralph loops and the two-part harness are both reset-based designs.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>In practice\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The same ideas run through every long-running harness you will meet: state on disk rather than in the conversation, one unit of work per session, a smoke test before new work, verification as a user would do it, and commits as checkpoints. Splitting the work across specialised agents (a planner, a generator, a sceptical evaluator) is the next step, covered in the Multi-agent module; making the driver itself crash-proof with durable execution is covered in the Graphs module.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">What does the initializer session produce, and why is it a separate prompt?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The environment every later session depends on: a comprehensive feature list with everything marked failing, an \u003ccode>init.sh\u003c/code> that starts the app, a progress file and an initial git commit. It needs a different job description from the coding sessions, which must work incrementally on one feature rather than set things up.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why does each coding session run a basic end-to-end test before starting a new feature?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>To detect whether the previous session left the app broken. Fixing that first stops new work from being built on top of a broken state, which would make the problem harder to find and fix.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">How does the harness stop the agent from declaring the project finished too early?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The feature list defines \"finished\" as every feature passing, starting with all of them failing; the agent may only flip a feature's \u003ccode>passes\u003c/code> field after testing it end to end, and the driver checks the list rather than trusting the agent's statement.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","l:loops/outer-loops-improving-the-agent-itself":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>The \u003cb>inner loop\u003c/b> is the agent working on a task. The \u003cb>outer loop\u003c/b> is you improving the agent: collect traces, read them, name the failure modes, change one thing in the harness, re-run the evals and keep the change only if the numbers improve. Loop engineering is mostly outer-loop work, and the teams with the best agents run it every week.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>The outer loop\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"flow\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Diagram\">\u003cdiv class=\"fr\">\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Run on real and eval tasks\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Read traces\u003c/b>\u003csmall>label failures\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Pick the top failure mode\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Change one thing\u003c/b>\u003csmall>tool, prompt, check, budget\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Re-run evals\u003c/b>\u003csmall>several trials\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Keep or revert\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Add the case to the eval set\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Five habits make it work:\u003c/p>\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cb>Trace everything.\u003c/b> Every model call, tool call, result, token count, cost and stop status, per run. Anthropic's eval vocabulary is useful here: a \u003cb>transcript\u003c/b> (the full record of a trial) is not the same as the \u003cb>outcome\u003c/b> (the final state of the environment). An agent can say \"your flight is booked\" while no booking exists.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Read before you measure.\u003c/b> Read 20 to 50 traces and write a one-line label for each failure. Group them. The biggest group is your next fix. (The Evals module covers error analysis in depth.)\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Fix at the right layer.\u003c/b> Most failures are not \"the prompt is bad\".\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Change one thing at a time\u003c/b> and compare on the same eval set, with several trials per task because agents vary between runs.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Every fixed failure becomes an eval case\u003c/b>, so it cannot quietly return.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003ch4>Where to fix what\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Failure mode in traces\u003c/th>\u003cth>Layer\u003c/th>\u003cth>Typical fix\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Picks the wrong tool\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Tools\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Clearer names and descriptions; merge overlapping tools\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Malformed or wrong arguments\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Tools\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Tighter schemas, examples in descriptions, errors that show the right format\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Repeats a failing call\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Errors and loop guard\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Better error text; repeat detection\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Claims done when it is not\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Verification\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Done check or Stop hook with a real test\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Loses track in long tasks\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Context and state\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Plan file, smaller units of work, resets with handoff notes\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Ignores a rule\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Instructions or hooks\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Move the rule to an instruction file that is always loaded; enforce hard rules with a hook\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Invents facts\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Retrieval and checks\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Ground with retrieval; verify citations in code\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Costs too much\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Economics\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Cache-friendly layout, smaller model for subtasks, trimmed tool output\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>A rule of thumb from Claude Code's guidance: rules that must never have exceptions belong in \u003cb>hooks or permissions\u003c/b>, enforced by the harness, not in prose the model may forget.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Measure what matters\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Success on outcomes\u003c/b>, graded by checking the environment.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Consistency.\u003c/b> pass@k (at least one of k trials succeeds) rises with k; pass^k (all k succeed) falls. An agent with a 75% per-trial success rate passes three trials in a row only about 42% of the time (0.75 cubed). Customer-facing agents need pass^k.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Cost per successful task\u003c/b>, steps and latency. A change that lifts success from 80% to 82% while doubling cost may not be worth it.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp>A minimal script to start error analysis from JSON Lines traces:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>import collections, json, pathlib\n\nstatuses, tool_errors = collections.Counter(), collections.Counter()\nfor path in pathlib.Path(\"runs\").glob(\"*.jsonl\"):\n    events = [json.loads(line) for line in path.open()]\n    statuses[events[-1].get(\"status\", \"unknown\")] += 1\n    for e in events:\n        if e.get(\"type\") == \"tool_result\" and str(e.get(\"content\", \"\")).startswith(\"Error\"):\n            tool_errors[e.get(\"tool\", \"?\")] += 1\nprint(\"final statuses:\", statuses.most_common())\nprint(\"tools with most errors:\", tool_errors.most_common(5))\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Let agents help, carefully\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Anthropic describes using Claude Code to analyse eval transcripts and rewrite tool descriptions and implementations, then re-running the eval to confirm the improvement. That works because the eval is the judge. Automated prompt optimisers (DSPy, covered in the Frameworks module) follow the same principle. Without a trustworthy eval, an agent \"improving\" your agent is just another unverified loop.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Harness changes have a shelf life\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Harness features encode what the current model cannot do. When you change models, re-run the eval with and without each piece of scaffolding: Anthropic found context resets essential for one model and dead weight for its successor. Deleting scaffolding that no longer earns its keep is outer-loop work too.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Your agent's success rate dropped after a change. Describe the outer-loop steps you would take.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Compare traces of failing tasks before and after the change and label the new failure modes; check whether the outcome (environment state) or only the transcript changed; locate the layer responsible (tools, instructions, checks, context or budgets); revert or adjust that one change; re-run the eval with several trials per task and compare success, consistency and cost per success; add the newly failing cases to the eval set.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why distinguish a transcript from an outcome when grading agents?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The transcript is what the agent said and did; the outcome is the actual final state of the environment. Agents can claim success that did not happen, or reach the right state by an unexpected route, so grading the outcome measures what users care about.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">An agent succeeds on a task 90% of the time per trial. What is its pass^3, and why might that matter?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>0.9 cubed is about 0.73, so all three trials succeed only 73% of the time. For a customer-facing agent that users rely on every time, consistency across runs is what they experience, so pass^k is the more honest number.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","l:loops/loop-economics":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>An agent loop re-sends its whole history on every turn, so input tokens grow roughly with the \u003cb>square\u003c/b> of the number of steps. Prompt caching turns most of that re-sent prefix into cheap cache reads, which can cut a loop's cost by around three quarters or more, but only if the prefix stays stable. Cap cost with budgets per run, limits on tool output and alerts on cost per successful task.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Why loops are quadratic\u003c/h4>\u003cp>If the fixed prefix (system prompt, tool definitions, instructions) is \u003ccode>B\u003c/code> tokens and each step adds \u003ccode>G\u003c/code> tokens (the model's tool call plus the tool result), then step \u003ccode>k\u003c/code> sends about \u003ccode>B + G×k\u003c/code> input tokens. Over \u003ccode>n\u003c/code> steps:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>total input ≈ n × B + G × n(n - 1) / 2\n\nExample: B = 5,000, G = 1,500, n = 30 steps\ntotal input = 30 × 5,000 + 1,500 × 435 = 150,000 + 652,500 = 802,500 tokens\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The final request is only about 48,500 tokens, yet the run sends over 800,000. Doubling the steps roughly quadruples the cost.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>A worked example\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Illustrative prices: $3 per million input tokens, $15 per million output tokens, cache writes at 1.25 times the input price and cache reads at 0.1 times (common ratios; check your provider and model, since some late-2026 models charge even less for reads). Assume 300 output tokens per step.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>Without caching\n  input   802,500 × $3 / 1M                              = $2.41\n  output  30 × 300 × $15 / 1M                            = $0.14\n  total                                                  = $2.54\n\nWith caching (each turn's new tokens are written once, everything before them is read)\n  written 5,000 + 29 × 1,500 = 48,500 × 1.25 = 60,625 token-equivalents\n  read    802,500 - 48,500   = 754,000 × 0.1 = 75,400 token-equivalents\n  input   136,025 × $3 / 1M                              = $0.41\n  total   $0.41 + $0.14                                  = $0.54   (about 79% cheaper)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Keeping the cache warm in a loop\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Caching works on the \u003cb>exact prefix\u003c/b> of the request, in order: tools, then system, then messages (the Billing module covers the details). A loop that only appends to the end gets a cache hit on everything before the newest messages. Things that silently break it:\u003c/p>\u003cul>\u003cli>Changing the tool list or tool order mid-run, or switching model.\u003c/li>\u003cli>A timestamp, random id or running counter in the system prompt.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Editing or deleting earlier messages (compaction and clearing old tool results do this deliberately; do it rarely and at clear boundaries).\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Time.\u003c/b> Anthropic's default cache lives 5 minutes from the start of the request that created it, refreshed on each hit. A ten-minute test run inside one step lets it expire; a longer-lived cache option exists at a higher write price. OpenAI caches automatically, for prompts of at least 1,024 tokens on current models.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp>The Claude Agent SDK notes the same: content that stays the same across turns (system prompt, tool definitions, instruction files) is prompt-cached automatically, while large tool outputs accumulate and stay in context for every later turn.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>What a Ralph iteration costs\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Fresh-context loops trade cache efficiency for clean context. Suppose each Ralph iteration loads 25,000 tokens of prompt, specs and plan, runs 30 internal turns adding 2,200 tokens each and writes 400 output tokens per turn. Using the same formula and prices, an iteration sends about 1.7 million input tokens: roughly \u003cb>$5.30 without caching or $1.00 with it\u003c/b>. Thirty iterations overnight: about $30 with caching, about $160 without. That is cheap compared with engineering time, but only if the loop is making progress, which is why the stuck detectors in the Ralph lesson matter.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Where cost blows up\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Cause\u003c/th>\u003cth>Why it hurts\u003c/th>\u003cth>Cap or fix\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>No step or spend limit\u003c/td>\u003ctd>One stuck run loops all night\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>max_turns\u003c/code>, dollar budget per run, iteration cap\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Huge tool outputs\u003c/td>\u003ctd>A 20,000-token file read stays in every later request\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Truncate, paginate, return ids; Claude Code caps tool responses at 25,000 tokens by default\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Cache misses\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Every turn pays full input price\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Stable prefix; no timestamps up front; append only\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>High reasoning effort everywhere\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Thinking tokens are billed as output\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Lower effort for routine steps\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Fan-out to subagents\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Each subagent has its own growing context\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Cap the number; cheaper models; short briefs\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Retrying long calls\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Pays for the whole context again\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Retry only transient errors, with backoff\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Many loops in parallel\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Multiplies everything\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Organisation-wide spend limits and alerts\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>In practice\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Track \u003cb>cost per successful task\u003c/b>, not cost per run: a cheap agent that fails half the time can cost more per success than an expensive one that rarely fails. Set budgets in three places: per run (in the harness), per day or project (in the gateway or provider console) and per experiment (in your loop script). Put the stable parts of the context first, keep tool outputs small, and use a smaller model or lower effort for steps that do not need the best one.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">An agent loop with a 4,000-token prefix adds 1,000 tokens per step. Roughly how many input tokens do 20 steps send, and what happens if you double to 40 steps?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>20 × 4,000 + 1,000 × (20 × 19 / 2) = 80,000 + 190,000 = 270,000 tokens. At 40 steps: 160,000 + 1,000 × 780 = 940,000 tokens, about 3.5 times as much for twice the steps, because the growing history is re-sent every turn.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Name three things that break prompt caching inside an agent loop.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Any three of: changing tools, tool order or model mid-run; dynamic content such as timestamps early in the prompt; editing or removing earlier messages (including compaction); long pauses that let the cache expire before the next request.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why is cost per successful task a better metric than cost per run?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Failed runs still cost money and produce nothing. An agent that costs $0.20 per run with 50% success costs $0.40 per success, more than one costing $0.30 per run with 95% success (about $0.32 per success).\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","l:loops/loop-safety":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>An agent loop runs code and commands chosen by a model that can make mistakes and can be manipulated by text it reads. Make it safe in layers: a \u003cb>sandbox\u003c/b> that limits what it can touch, \u003cb>network isolation\u003c/b> that limits where data can go, \u003cb>permissions\u003c/b> and allowlists for what it may run, \u003cb>scoped credentials\u003c/b>, \u003cb>budgets and a kill switch\u003c/b>, and \u003cb>logs\u003c/b> you review. Unattended loops need all of them, because nobody is watching when something goes wrong.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>What can go wrong\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Honest mistakes:\u003c/b> \u003ccode>rm -rf\u003c/code> in the wrong directory, \u003ccode>git push --force\u003c/code>, dropping a table, filling the disk.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Prompt injection:\u003c/b> an issue, web page, README or dependency file contains instructions the agent follows.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Credential exposure:\u003c/b> the agent reads \u003ccode>~/.ssh\u003c/code>, cloud credentials or environment variables and they end up in logs, commits or requests.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Exfiltration:\u003c/b> the lethal trifecta of private data, untrusted content and a way to send data out.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Runaway resources:\u003c/b> infinite loops, fork bombs, huge bills.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Supply chain:\u003c/b> the agent installs a malicious or typo-squatted package.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>The layers\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Layer\u003c/th>\u003cth>What it does\u003c/th>\u003cth>Tools and settings\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Sandbox\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Only the project is visible and writable; no host access\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Container or VM, dev container, OS sandbox runtime; non-root user; CPU, memory and process limits\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Network isolation\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Deny by default; allow only what the task needs\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Egress proxy with a domain allowlist (model API, package mirror)\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Permissions\u003c/td>\u003ctd>What the agent may run without asking\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Claude Code modes (\u003ccode>default\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>acceptEdits\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>plan\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>dontAsk\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>auto\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>bypassPermissions\u003c/code>); Codex sandbox modes (\u003ccode>read-only\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>workspace-write\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>danger-full-access\u003c/code>); allow and deny rules\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Hooks\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Your code vets each action\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>PreToolUse\u003c/code> hooks that block patterns or paths\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Credentials\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Least privilege, short-lived\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Bot accounts, tokens scoped to one repository, no production secrets, push to branches only\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Limits and kill switch\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Bound damage and cost\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Turn, time and dollar caps; a STOP file; stop the container\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Review\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Humans see what happened\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Full logs, diffs reviewed before merge, protected main branch\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Anthropic's point about the first two layers is worth memorising: \u003cb>effective sandboxing needs both\u003c/b>. Without network isolation, a compromised agent can send out your SSH keys; without filesystem isolation, it can escape the sandbox and reach the network. In their internal use, sandboxing also cut permission prompts by 84%, because inside safe boundaries the agent can work without asking.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Permission modes\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The Claude Agent SDK documentation describes the trade-off clearly: \u003ccode>default\u003c/code> asks your approval callback for anything not pre-allowed (and denies if there is none); \u003ccode>acceptEdits\u003c/code> auto-approves file edits and common filesystem commands while other shell commands still need allow rules; \u003ccode>plan\u003c/code> explores without editing; \u003ccode>dontAsk\u003c/code> never prompts and denies anything not pre-approved, which suits headless agents; \u003ccode>auto\u003c/code> uses a classifier to review risky actions; and \u003ccode>bypassPermissions\u003c/code> runs everything and should be used \u003cb>only in isolated environments\u003c/b> such as CI containers (it cannot be used as root on Unix). For an unattended loop on a dev machine, \u003ccode>acceptEdits\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>dontAsk\u003c/code> with a short allowlist is the usual choice; save bypass mode for a disposable container.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>A container and a hook\u003c/h4>\u003cp>A container that limits what a loop can do (the network named \u003ccode>agent-egress\u003c/code> is assumed to route through a proxy that allows only your model API and package mirror):\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">bash\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>docker run --rm \\\n  --user 1000:1000 --cpus 2 --memory 4g --pids-limit 256 \\\n  --cap-drop ALL --security-opt no-new-privileges \\\n  --network agent-egress \\\n  -v \"$PWD\":/work -w /work \\\n  -e ANTHROPIC_API_KEY \\\n  ralph-image ./ralph.sh\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>A Claude Code \u003ccode>PreToolUse\u003c/code> hook that blocks some dangerous commands. Exit code 2 blocks the call and sends stderr back to the agent as the reason:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">json\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>{\"hooks\": {\"PreToolUse\": [{\"matcher\": \"Bash\",\n  \"hooks\": [{\"type\": \"command\", \"command\": \"${CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR}/.claude/hooks/guard.sh\"}]}]}}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">bash\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>#!/bin/bash\n# .claude/hooks/guard.sh: reads the tool call as JSON on stdin\nCMD=$(jq -r '.tool_input.command')\nif echo \"$CMD\" | grep -Eq 'rm -rf|git push --force|curl |wget |ssh |scp '; then\n  echo \"Blocked by policy: $CMD\" &gt;&amp;2\n  exit 2\nfi\nexit 0\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>A deny list like this is easy to get round (\u003ccode>rm -r -f\u003c/code>, a Python one-liner), so treat hooks as a seatbelt on top of the sandbox and allowlists, never as the only barrier.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Unattended-run checklist\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Before you leave a loop running overnight:\u003c/p>\u003col>\u003cli>It runs in a container or VM with only the project mounted, as a non-root user.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Outbound network is limited to an allowlist.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Credentials are scoped bot tokens; no personal keys, cloud admin roles or production secrets are reachable.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Git pushes go to a branch; main is protected and needs review.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Iterations, turns, wall-clock time and dollars are capped, and a provider-level spend limit exists.\u003c/li>\u003cli>There is a kill switch you can trigger from your phone (a STOP file in a synced folder, or stopping the container).\u003c/li>\u003cli>Every iteration writes logs and a diff you can review in the morning.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Untrusted inputs (issues, web pages) are treated as data, and the agent cannot both read them and send data to arbitrary destinations.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why does a sandbox need network isolation as well as filesystem isolation?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Filesystem isolation alone still lets a manipulated agent send whatever it can read (keys, source code, data) to an attacker's server; network isolation alone lets it modify or read sensitive files and possibly escape to reach the network. Together they limit both what it can touch and where anything can go.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Which permission mode would you use for an unattended loop on a development machine, and which only in a disposable container?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>On a development machine, \u003ccode>acceptEdits\u003c/code> (or \u003ccode>dontAsk\u003c/code>) with a short allowlist of commands such as the test runner and git commit, so edits are free but other commands are denied. \u003ccode>bypassPermissions\u003c/code> only in an isolated, disposable environment such as a CI container, because it runs every tool call without checks.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why is a PreToolUse deny-list hook not enough on its own?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Pattern matching on command strings is easy to bypass with equivalent commands (different flags, another language, an encoded script), so it catches mistakes rather than determined misuse. The real boundaries are the sandbox, network allowlist, credential scope and permission rules; the hook is an extra layer.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","lab:loops/a-verify-fix-loop":"\u003cp>You will write a small harness around a model that fixes a deliberately buggy module. Everything from this module's lessons appears in about 100 lines: the loop, tool dispatch, errors as observations, a budget, a protected check, done verification and stuck detection.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Setup\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Python 3.11+, \u003ccode>pip install openai pytest\u003c/code>, and git\u003c/li>\u003cli>An API key for any OpenAI-compatible provider, or a local model with tool calling via Ollama\u003c/li>\u003cli>A working folder containing a project folder \u003ccode>textlab/\u003c/code>. The harness lives \u003cb>outside\u003c/b> the project, so the agent cannot edit its own harness.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp>Create \u003ccode>textlab/textstats.py\u003c/code> (three bugs on purpose):\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>import re\nfrom collections import Counter\n\ndef word_count(text: str) -&gt; int:\n    return len(text.split(\" \"))\n\ndef top_words(text: str, n: int = 3) -&gt; list[tuple[str, int]]:\n    words = re.findall(r\"[a-z']+\", text)\n    return Counter(words).most_common(n)\n\ndef slugify(title: str) -&gt; str:\n    return title.lower().replace(\" \", \"-\")\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Create \u003ccode>textlab/tests/test_textstats.py\u003c/code>:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>from textstats import slugify, top_words, word_count\n\ndef test_word_count_basic():\n    assert word_count(\"the quick brown fox\") == 4\n\ndef test_word_count_whitespace():\n    assert word_count(\"  two   words\\n\") == 2\n\ndef test_word_count_empty():\n    assert word_count(\"\") == 0\n\ndef test_top_words_ignores_case():\n    assert top_words(\"The cat and THE dog and the bird\", 2) == [(\"the\", 3), (\"and\", 2)]\n\ndef test_slugify_punctuation():\n    assert slugify(\"Hello, World!\") == \"hello-world\"\n\ndef test_slugify_spaces():\n    assert slugify(\"  Loop   engineering 101 \") == \"loop-engineering-101\"\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Steps\u003c/h4>\u003col>\u003cli>In \u003ccode>textlab/\u003c/code>, run \u003ccode>python -m pytest -q\u003c/code>. You should see \u003cb>5 failed, 1 passed\u003c/b>. Then \u003ccode>git init &amp;&amp; git add -A &amp;&amp; git commit -m \"buggy start\"\u003c/code> so you can reset between runs.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Next to \u003ccode>textlab/\u003c/code> (not inside it), save \u003ccode>verify_fix.py\u003c/code>:\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>import json, subprocess, sys\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfrom openai import OpenAI\n\nclient = OpenAI()                    # set base_url and api_key for your provider\nMODEL = \"&lt;model&gt;\"\nROOT = Path(sys.argv[1] if len(sys.argv) &gt; 1 else \"textlab\").resolve()\nMAX_STEPS, MAX_TEST_RUNS = 25, 10\n\ndef safe_path(rel: str) -&gt; Path:\n    p = (ROOT / rel).resolve()\n    if not p.is_relative_to(ROOT):\n        raise ValueError(f\"{rel} is outside the project\")\n    return p\n\ndef list_files() -&gt; str:\n    files = (p.relative_to(ROOT) for p in sorted(ROOT.rglob(\"*.py\")))\n    return \"\\n\".join(str(f) for f in files\n                     if not any(part.startswith(\".\") or part == \"__pycache__\" for part in f.parts))\n\ndef read_file(path: str) -&gt; str:\n    return safe_path(path).read_text()[:10_000]\n\ndef write_file(path: str, content: str) -&gt; str:\n    p = safe_path(path)\n    if p.is_relative_to(ROOT / \"tests\"):\n        return \"Error: tests/ is read-only. Change the code, not the tests.\"\n    p.write_text(content)\n    return f\"Wrote {len(content)} characters to {path}.\"\n\ndef run_tests() -&gt; str:\n    p = subprocess.run([sys.executable, \"-m\", \"pytest\", \"-q\", \"--tb=short\", \"-p\", \"no:cacheprovider\"],\n                       cwd=ROOT, capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=120)\n    return (\"PASSED\" if p.returncode == 0 else \"FAILED\") + \"\\n\" + (p.stdout + p.stderr)[-2500:]\n\ndef failing(report: str) -&gt; frozenset:\n    return frozenset(l.split(\" - \")[0] for l in report.splitlines() if l.startswith(\"FAILED \"))\n\nIMPL = {\"list_files\": list_files, \"read_file\": read_file, \"write_file\": write_file, \"run_tests\": run_tests}\n\ndef tool(name: str, description: str, **params: str) -&gt; dict:\n    return {\"type\": \"function\", \"function\": {\"name\": name, \"description\": description, \"parameters\": {\n        \"type\": \"object\", \"required\": list(params),\n        \"properties\": {k: {\"type\": \"string\", \"description\": v} for k, v in params.items()}}}}\n\nTOOLS = [\n    tool(\"list_files\", \"List the Python files in the project.\"),\n    tool(\"read_file\", \"Read a file. Paths are relative to the project root.\", path=\"for example textstats.py\"),\n    tool(\"write_file\", \"Replace the whole content of a file.\", path=\"file to write\",\n         content=\"the complete new content of the file\"),\n    tool(\"run_tests\", \"Run the test suite. Returns PASSED or FAILED and the end of the pytest output.\"),\n]\n\nSYSTEM = \"\"\"You fix a small Python project until its test suite passes.\nLoop: run the tests, read the code that fails, write a fix, run the tests again.\nNever modify files in tests/. Do not special-case test inputs: fix the real behaviour.\nWhen the tests pass, reply with a short summary of your changes and make no tool call.\"\"\"\n\ndef main() -&gt; None:\n    messages = [{\"role\": \"system\", \"content\": SYSTEM},\n                {\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"The test suite is failing. Make it pass.\"}]\n    test_runs, tokens, last_failing, repeats = 0, 0, None, 0\n    for step in range(1, MAX_STEPS + 1):\n        r = client.chat.completions.create(model=MODEL, messages=messages, tools=TOOLS)\n        tokens += r.usage.total_tokens if r.usage else 0\n        msg = r.choices[0].message\n        messages.append(msg.model_dump(exclude_none=True))\n        if not msg.tool_calls:                                  # a claim of done: verify it\n            report = IMPL[\"run_tests\"]()\n            if report.startswith(\"PASSED\"):\n                print(f\"DONE after {step} steps and {tokens} tokens.\\n{msg.content}\")\n                return\n            messages.append({\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"The tests still fail:\\n\" + report})\n            continue\n        for call in msg.tool_calls:\n            name = call.function.name\n            try:\n                args = json.loads(call.function.arguments or \"{}\")\n                if name == \"run_tests\":\n                    test_runs += 1\n                    if test_runs &gt; MAX_TEST_RUNS:\n                        print(\"STOP: test-run budget used up.\"); return\n                result = IMPL[name](**args) if name in IMPL else f\"Error: no tool named {name}\"\n            except Exception as e:\n                result = f\"Error: {type(e).__name__}: {e}\"\n            if name == \"run_tests\" and result.startswith(\"FAILED\"):\n                now = failing(result)\n                repeats = repeats + 1 if now == last_failing else 0\n                last_failing = now\n                if repeats &gt;= 2:\n                    print(\"STOP: the same tests failed three runs in a row (stuck).\"); return\n            print(f\"step {step:2}  {name:10}  {str(result).splitlines()[0][:70]}\")\n            messages.append({\"role\": \"tool\", \"tool_call_id\": call.id, \"content\": str(result)})\n    print(f\"STOP: step budget of {MAX_STEPS} used up after {tokens} tokens.\")\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003col start=\"3\">\u003cli>Run \u003ccode>python verify_fix.py textlab\u003c/code>. Watch each step print. When it finishes, run \u003ccode>git -C textlab diff\u003c/code> and read the fix: is it a real fix or a special case?\u003c/li>\u003cli>Reset with \u003ccode>git -C textlab checkout -- .\u003c/code> and set \u003ccode>MAX_STEPS = 3\u003c/code>. Run again and confirm it stops with the step-budget message instead of a fake answer.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Reset, restore \u003ccode>MAX_STEPS\u003c/code>, and test the guard: add an impossible test, \u003ccode>assert word_count(\"a b\") == 3\u003c/code>, to the test file and commit it. Run the harness. Watch for attempts to write to \u003ccode>tests/\u003c/code> (refused) and for special-casing. It should end with the stuck message or the test-run budget. Then remove the impossible test.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Temporarily delete the \u003ccode>tests/\u003c/code> check in \u003ccode>write_file\u003c/code>, keep the impossible test, and run once more. Does the model edit or delete the test to \"pass\"? Restore the check and remove the test afterwards.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Switch \u003ccode>MODEL\u003c/code> to a smaller or local model and compare steps, tokens and the quality of the diff.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003ch4>What you should see\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>A capable model usually runs the tests, reads \u003ccode>textstats.py\u003c/code>, writes one fixed version and passes within a handful of steps. A typical fix uses \u003ccode>text.split()\u003c/code>, lower-cases the text in \u003ccode>top_words\u003c/code> and uses a regular expression in \u003ccode>slugify\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Tokens per step grow as the history grows, even though each step is small.\u003c/li>\u003cli>With an impossible test, the harness stops with a clear status instead of spinning to the step limit, and protected tests stay untouched.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Without the guard, some models edit the test or hard-code the expected value. That is the reward hacking the Verification lesson warned about, and why the guard lives in the harness rather than the prompt.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Smaller models take more steps, sometimes declare \"done\" too early (the verified done check catches this) and sometimes get stuck.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why does the harness live outside the project folder?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The agent's tools can only reach files under \u003ccode>ROOT\u003c/code>, so it cannot edit its own harness, budgets or guards. A harness the agent can modify is not a boundary.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">What happens when the model replies without tool calls but the tests fail, and why is that important?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The harness runs the tests itself, sends the failing output back as a new message and continues. The model's \"done\" is treated as a claim to verify, so premature completion becomes another iteration instead of a wrong result.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","b:loops/ralph-style-coding-loop-with-guardrails":"\u003ch4>The brief\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Build \u003ccode>mdlinks\u003c/code>, a command-line tool that scans a folder of Markdown files and reports broken internal links: missing files, missing heading anchors and links that escape the folder. You will not write the tool's code yourself. You will write the specs and acceptance tests, then build and run a Ralph loop with guardrails that writes the tool, and tune the loop until it finishes. The real deliverable is the loop, its logs and your write-up of what you changed and why. This is a standalone project, separate from Atlas.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Requirements\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003ccode>specs/\u003c/code> with three to five spec files (command-line interface, link extraction, anchor rules, output formats including JSON, exit codes), written in a conversation with an agent and reviewed by you line by line\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ccode>acceptance/\u003c/code> with 10 to 20 black-box tests you write before looping: they run the CLI on fixture folders and check output and exit codes. The agent must never be able to change them.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ccode>PROMPT_plan.md\u003c/code> (builds or rebuilds \u003ccode>fix_plan.md\u003c/code> from specs and code) and \u003ccode>PROMPT_build.md\u003c/code> (one item per iteration, tests, commit), plus \u003ccode>AGENTS.md\u003c/code> with build and test commands\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ccode>ralph.sh\u003c/code> with: an iteration cap; a per-iteration timeout, turn limit and dollar budget; a running total of cost with an overall cap; a STOP-file kill switch; a DONE file; an acceptance-test hash check; stop after three iterations without a commit; stop after three iterations in a row with failing acceptance tests; one log file per iteration\u003c/li>\u003cli>The loop runs in a container or VM with only the project mounted, as a non-root user, with outbound network limited to the model API (or inside your agent CLI's own sandbox mode with network disabled)\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ccode>report.py\u003c/code> that reads the logs and prints a table per iteration (commit, acceptance tests passing, turns, cost, stop reason) and totals\u003c/li>\u003cli>A write-up of one to two pages\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Milestones\u003c/h4>\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cb>Specs (about 1 h).\u003c/b> Discuss the tool with an agent until it understands the requirements, then ask it to write one spec per topic. Read every line; fix contradictions now, because the loop will faithfully build whatever the specs say.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Acceptance tests (1 to 1.5 h).\u003c/b> Create fixture folders (good links, missing file, missing anchor, link outside the root, headings with punctuation) and pytest tests that run \u003ccode>python -m mdlinks &lt;folder&gt;\u003c/code> via \u003ccode>subprocess\u003c/code> and assert on output and exit code. All should fail now.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Prompts and skeleton (about 1 h).\u003c/b> Create the package skeleton with pytest and ruff configured, \u003ccode>AGENTS.md\u003c/code>, both prompts and a first \u003ccode>fix_plan.md\u003c/code> from one run of the planning prompt. Commit.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>The loop and its guardrails (1 to 1.5 h).\u003c/b> Write \u003ccode>ralph.sh\u003c/code> starting from the version in the Ralph lesson. Trigger every guardrail on purpose: create STOP, set a tiny budget, edit an acceptance test, force an iteration with no commit. Each must stop the loop with a clear log line.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Sandbox (0.5 to 1 h).\u003c/b> Run the loop in a container (see the Loop safety lesson) or the agent CLI's sandbox, with scoped credentials and no access to your home directory.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Attended run (1 to 2 h).\u003c/b> Run about ten iterations while watching the logs. Every time the agent does something wrong, add a sign to the prompt, a line to \u003ccode>AGENTS.md\u003c/code>, a check to the loop, or fix a spec. Record each change and the log line that prompted it.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Unattended run.\u003c/b> Let it run to DONE or to a cap. Do not intervene; read the logs afterwards.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Report and write-up (about 1 h).\u003c/b> Run \u003ccode>report.py\u003c/code>, then write up: final acceptance pass rate, total cost and iterations, cost per passing acceptance test, the signs you added and why, failures the guardrails caught, and what you would change.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003ch4>Done when\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>All acceptance tests pass, or the loop stopped for a recorded reason that your write-up explains\u003c/li>\u003cli>Every guardrail has been triggered deliberately at least once and stopped the loop as intended\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ccode>python report.py logs/\u003c/code> prints the per-iteration table and the totals\u003c/li>\u003cli>The write-up lists at least five tuning changes, each with the log evidence that prompted it\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Stretch goals\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Every fifth iteration, run a reviewer session instead of a builder: a fresh agent that reads the recent diffs, hunts for placeholders, skipped tests and duplicated code, and adds findings to \u003ccode>fix_plan.md\u003c/code>\u003c/li>\u003cli>Add a Stop hook (or equivalent) that runs ruff and a type checker before an iteration may end\u003c/li>\u003cli>Compare two models or two agent CLIs on cost per passing acceptance test and number of iterations\u003c/li>\u003cli>Rebuild the plan with the planning prompt whenever three iterations in a row fail to commit, instead of stopping\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Hints\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>With \u003ccode>claude -p --output-format stream-json --verbose\u003c/code>, the last JSON line of each session is a result message carrying \u003ccode>total_cost_usd\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>num_turns\u003c/code> and a \u003ccode>subtype\u003c/code> such as \u003ccode>success\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>error_max_turns\u003c/code>. Parse the log line by line and skip lines that are not JSON.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Keep \u003ccode>PROMPT_build.md\u003c/code> short and stable; put project-specific commands in \u003ccode>AGENTS.md\u003c/code>, and tell the agent to keep \u003ccode>AGENTS.md\u003c/code> free of status reports, as Huntley does.\u003c/li>\u003cli>If the loop keeps choosing big items, split them in \u003ccode>fix_plan.md\u003c/code> yourself: \"one item per loop\" only works if items are small.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ccode>git reset --hard\u003c/code> to the last good commit is a legitimate move. Huntley describes waking up to a broken build and deciding whether to reset or to write prompts that rescue it.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>"}]);
